    Warm the V4.1 Engram page cache when the tables fit in memory
    
    Prefill reads 2 x 24 Engram rows per token, spread across 189 GiB. That
    random pattern is the worst case for external SSDs: a USB drive served
    about 6K rows/s over the whole span, uncached or buffered alike, so a 2K
    prompt stalled about 12 s before layers 1 and 14. Q2 single-Mac prefill
    ran at 205-290 t/s while its GPU work alone supported ~420.
    
    When the resident model, graph buffers, a 64 GiB reserve and the tables
    fit in physical memory (Q2 on a 512 GB Mac; not Q4), a low-priority
    background reader streams the tables once into the kernel page cache at
    the drive's sequential rate (~1 GB/s over USB, about 3.5 minutes). The
    uncached row readers then hit memory. No table is allocated or wired, the
    pages stay reclaimable, and the warmer stops at memory pressure. The same
    bytes are read either way, so logits do not change.
    
    Q2 from a USB SSD, cold prompts: 2K 198 -> 420 t/s, 8K 214 -> 518 t/s.
    DS4_DISABLE_V41_ENGRAM_WARM=1 skips the warmer; DS4_V41_ENGRAM_WARM_WAIT=1
    blocks until it finishes, for benchmarks.

